1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,230
Most WSET Level 2 questions link three things: a grape, a region and a style.

2
00:00:05,230 --> 00:00:09,182
Learn those links, and you can answer a large part of the exam.

3
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:18,161
In this video we go through the principal grapes one by one: where they grow, what their wines taste like, and how a cool or a warm climate changes them.

4
00:00:18,161 --> 00:00:20,644
At the end, a quick quiz to check what stuck.

5
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:24,374
For every grape, learn three things.

6
00:00:24,374 --> 00:00:27,945
First, its key regions: where its best-known wines come from.

7
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:32,506
Second, its typical style: acidity, tannin, body and the kind of fruit.

8
00:00:33,053 --> 00:00:35,024
And third, how climate changes it.

9
00:00:35,024 --> 00:00:38,153
This last one is where many exam questions are hiding.

10
00:00:39,267 --> 00:00:40,313
Chapter one.

11
00:00:40,313 --> 00:00:42,143
Cool or warm climate?

12
00:00:43,767 --> 00:00:48,815
A cool climate gives wines with higher acidity, a lighter body, and green or citrus fruit.

13
00:00:49,353 --> 00:00:52,634
A warm climate gives riper fruit, more body and more alcohol.

14
00:00:53,187 --> 00:01:00,881
Exam questions ask for exactly this contrast again and again, especially for Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Syrah and Cabernet Sauvignon.

15
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,932
It also helps to sort the regions.

16
00:01:03,932 --> 00:01:08,821
Cool: Chablis, Champagne, the Loire, the Mosel, Marlborough, Central Otago and Oregon.

17
00:01:09,353 --> 00:01:13,558
Moderate: Bordeaux, the Côte d'Or, the Northern Rhône, Rioja, Piedmont and Tuscany.

18
00:01:14,087 --> 00:01:18,254
Warm: the Southern Rhône, the Barossa, Napa and Chile's Central Valley.

19
00:01:18,254 --> 00:01:22,010
But watch out: altitude and sea breezes can cool a warm country.

20
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:26,001
Mendoza is cooled by height, and Casablanca in Chile by the Pacific.

21
00:01:27,133 --> 00:01:28,128
Chapter two.

22
00:01:28,128 --> 00:01:29,536
The white grapes.

23
00:01:31,167 --> 00:01:36,945
Chardonnay is a fairly neutral grape, so it shows the influence of climate and winemaking very clearly.

24
00:01:36,945 --> 00:01:41,208
Its key regions: Chablis and the rest of Burgundy, California and Australia.

25
00:01:41,753 --> 00:01:49,698
In Chablis, the coolest part of Burgundy, it gives lean wines with high acidity, green apple and lemon, usually with little or no new oak.

26
00:01:49,698 --> 00:01:54,246
In warm parts of California and Australia, it shows peach, melon and pineapple.

27
00:01:54,787 --> 00:02:00,823
Many of those warm-climate wines combine malolactic conversion with new oak, for butter, vanilla and toast.

28
00:02:00,823 --> 00:02:05,055
And remember: Chardonnay is also one of the three main grapes of Champagne.

29
00:02:06,167 --> 00:02:11,922
Sauvignon Blanc is aromatic, with high acidity and green notes: grass, green pepper and gooseberry.

30
00:02:11,922 --> 00:02:15,643
It is usually fermented cool in stainless steel and drunk young.

31
00:02:16,187 --> 00:02:20,776
In the Loire Valley, Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé give restrained, mineral, citrus styles.

32
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:26,701
Marlborough, in New Zealand, made it famous with a very intense, pungent style that adds passion fruit.

33
00:02:26,701 --> 00:02:31,194
In Bordeaux it is often blended with Sémillon, for dry whites and for sweet Sauternes.

34
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:37,392
Riesling is very aromatic, floral and citrus, and it keeps high acidity even when ripe.

35
00:02:37,392 --> 00:02:39,967
It is rarely oaked, so the fruit stays pure.

36
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:48,711
In the Mosel, a cool German region with steep slate slopes along the river, it gives light, low-alcohol wines, from dry to lusciously sweet.

37
00:02:48,711 --> 00:02:55,030
Alsace and Australia's Clare and Eden Valleys make mostly dry, fuller styles; the Australian ones with lime.

38
00:02:55,553 --> 00:03:02,576
Riesling ages very well, developing honey and toast, and it is used for late-harvest, noble rot and icewine sweet wines.

39
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:07,734
Pinot Grigio and Pinot Gris are the same grape with two personalities.

40
00:03:07,734 --> 00:03:13,497
As Pinot Grigio in north-east Italy it is picked early for light, crisp whites with citrus and pear.

41
00:03:13,497 --> 00:03:17,992
As Pinot Gris in Alsace it is picked riper, for fuller wines with stone fruit.

42
00:03:17,992 --> 00:03:20,240
The name on the label is a strong hint.

43
00:03:20,787 --> 00:03:24,696
Chenin Blanc has high acidity, which makes it extremely versatile.

44
00:03:24,696 --> 00:03:30,976
In the Loire, Vouvray comes in every style, from dry to sweet and sparkling, with apple, quince and honey.

45
00:03:30,976 --> 00:03:33,227
South Africa is the other key country.

46
00:03:34,333 --> 00:03:35,410
Chapter three.

47
00:03:35,410 --> 00:03:36,717
The black grapes.

48
00:03:38,333 --> 00:03:45,086
Pinot Noir has thin skins, so its wines are pale, with low to medium tannin and red fruit: strawberry, raspberry, cherry.

49
00:03:45,086 --> 00:03:47,709
With age it develops earthy and mushroom notes.

50
00:03:48,253 --> 00:03:51,749
It needs a cool climate; in heat it loses its delicacy.

51
00:03:51,749 --> 00:03:55,817
Burgundy, especially the Côte de Nuits, is its reference region.

52
00:03:56,353 --> 00:04:01,808
New Zealand, Oregon and the cooler parts of California and Chile make riper, fruitier versions.

53
00:04:01,808 --> 00:04:04,048
And it is a key grape in Champagne too.

54
00:04:05,167 --> 00:04:13,367
Cabernet Sauvignon has small, thick-skinned grapes: deep colour, high tannin, high acidity and blackcurrant, sometimes with green pepper and mint.

55
00:04:13,367 --> 00:04:15,108
It needs warmth to ripen fully.

56
00:04:15,653 --> 00:04:20,043
On Bordeaux's Left Bank, in the Médoc, gravel soils store heat and help it ripen.

57
00:04:20,043 --> 00:04:22,699
There it is blended with Merlot and other grapes.

58
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:27,441
Merlot ripens earlier and gives softer wines, with medium tannin and plum.

59
00:04:27,441 --> 00:04:29,894
One gives structure, the other gives flesh.

60
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:36,051
Merlot dominates the Right Bank, in Saint-Émilion and Pomerol, where cooler clay soils suit it better.

61
00:04:36,051 --> 00:04:41,460
Outside Bordeaux, think of Cabernet from Napa Valley and Coonawarra, and fruity Merlot from Chile.

62
00:04:42,567 --> 00:04:45,272
Syrah gives deep-coloured wines with black fruit.

63
00:04:45,272 --> 00:04:50,130
In the Northern Rhône, in Côte-Rôtie and Hermitage, it is structured, with black pepper.

64
00:04:50,130 --> 00:04:57,529
In Australia it is called Shiraz, and the warm Barossa Valley gives full-bodied wines with ripe blackberry, chocolate and sweet spice.

65
00:04:58,053 --> 00:05:03,349
Grenache loves heat and drought: high alcohol, light colour, ripe red fruit and soft tannins.

66
00:05:03,349 --> 00:05:08,758
It is the backbone of Southern Rhône blends like Châteauneuf-du-Pape, with Syrah and Mourvèdre.

67
00:05:08,758 --> 00:05:13,939
In Spain, as Garnacha, it appears in Rioja and Navarra, and makes powerful reds in Priorat.

68
00:05:15,067 --> 00:05:21,417
Tempranillo is Spain's leading quality black grape: medium to deep colour, red fruit, medium acidity and tannin.

69
00:05:21,953 --> 00:05:28,839
In Rioja it is often blended with Garnacha; in Ribera del Duero the style is usually deeper and more concentrated.

70
00:05:29,387 --> 00:05:31,264
It takes oak ageing very well.

71
00:05:31,264 --> 00:05:38,712
In Rioja, American oak brings vanilla and coconut, and you will see the ageing terms Crianza, Reserva and Gran Reserva.

72
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:45,234
Sangiovese is Tuscany's grape: high acidity, high tannin, and red cherry with herbal notes.

73
00:05:45,787 --> 00:05:51,966
It is the main grape of Chianti and Chianti Classico, and Brunello di Montalcino is made from Sangiovese alone.

74
00:05:52,487 --> 00:05:58,994
Nebbiolo, from Piedmont in north-west Italy, is a paradox: pale in colour, but very high in tannin and acidity.

75
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:05,442
Its two famous wines are Barolo and Barbaresco, with red cherry and roses that turn earthy over time.

76
00:06:05,442 --> 00:06:07,553
They need years of ageing to soften.

77
00:06:08,667 --> 00:06:12,697
Malbec gives deep-coloured wines with black fruit and medium to high tannin.

78
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:20,524
Its modern home is Mendoza, in Argentina, where high-altitude vineyards get sunny days and cool nights, keeping freshness and colour.

79
00:06:21,053 --> 00:06:25,412
Its historic home is Cahors, in south-west France, where it gives firm, dark wines.

80
00:06:26,533 --> 00:06:27,422
Chapter four.

81
00:06:27,422 --> 00:06:30,155
The regional grapes that keep coming up.

82
00:06:31,767 --> 00:06:36,004
Albariño, from Rías Baixas in Spain: high acidity, peach and citrus.

83
00:06:36,004 --> 00:06:40,304
Grüner Veltliner, from Austria: citrus, green fruit and white pepper.

84
00:06:40,853 --> 00:06:45,132
Furmint, from Tokaj in Hungary, famous for sweet noble rot wines.

85
00:06:45,132 --> 00:06:48,555
Torrontés, from Argentina: very floral and aromatic.

86
00:06:49,087 --> 00:06:52,818
Gewurztraminer, from Alsace: lychee and rose, with low acidity.

87
00:06:52,818 --> 00:06:57,793
And Viognier, from Condrieu in the Northern Rhône: apricot, blossom and a full body.

88
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:02,063
Gamay, in Beaujolais: light, red fruit, low tannin.

89
00:07:02,063 --> 00:07:05,163
Barbera, in Piedmont: high acidity and low tannin.

90
00:07:05,687 --> 00:07:08,744
Carmenère, in Chile: black fruit with green pepper.

91
00:07:08,744 --> 00:07:12,640
Pinotage, in South Africa: a crossing of Pinot Noir and Cinsault.

92
00:07:13,187 --> 00:07:17,356
Zinfandel, in California: very ripe, high in alcohol, with jammy fruit.

93
00:07:17,356 --> 00:07:20,821
And Montepulciano, in Abruzzo: deep colour and soft tannin.

94
00:07:21,933 --> 00:07:23,790
Some pairs are classic traps.

95
00:07:23,790 --> 00:07:29,358
Pouilly-Fumé is Loire Sauvignon Blanc; Pouilly-Fuissé is Chardonnay from the Mâconnais.

96
00:07:29,358 --> 00:07:37,231
Montepulciano d'Abruzzo is a grape in Abruzzo; Vino Nobile di Montepulciano is a Sangiovese wine named after a Tuscan town.

97
00:07:37,753 --> 00:07:41,991
Barolo is Nebbiolo from Piedmont; Brunello is Sangiovese from Tuscany.

98
00:07:41,991 --> 00:07:49,075
And Syrah and Shiraz are the same grape: the name usually signals a cooler, peppery style, or a riper Australian one.

99
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:51,144
Chapter five.

100
00:07:51,144 --> 00:07:52,087
A quick quiz.

101
00:07:52,087 --> 00:07:54,845
Pause the video if you need more time.

102
00:07:56,467 --> 00:07:57,399
Question one.

103
00:07:57,399 --> 00:07:59,693
Barolo is made from which grape?

104
00:07:59,693 --> 00:08:02,059
Sangiovese, Nebbiolo, or Barbera?

105
00:08:04,187 --> 00:08:04,782
Nebbiolo.

106
00:08:04,782 --> 00:08:08,224
Pale in colour, but very high in tannin and acidity.

107
00:08:09,333 --> 00:08:10,098
Question two.

108
00:08:10,098 --> 00:08:16,218
A pungent Sauvignon Blanc with passion fruit most likely comes from: Sancerre, Marlborough, or Bordeaux?

109
00:08:18,353 --> 00:08:20,007
Marlborough, in New Zealand.

110
00:08:20,007 --> 00:08:22,841
Sancerre is more restrained, mineral and citrus.

111
00:08:23,967 --> 00:08:24,854
Question three.

112
00:08:24,854 --> 00:08:27,753
Which description fits a cool-climate Chardonnay?

113
00:08:29,887 --> 00:08:32,427
High acidity, green apple and lemon.

114
00:08:32,427 --> 00:08:33,415
Think Chablis.

115
00:08:34,533 --> 00:08:38,273
To make it stick: group the grapes by colour and by the climate they prefer.

116
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:43,692
Practise in both directions: from the grape to the region, and from the region back to the grape.

117
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:47,750
And ten minutes of mixed questions every day beats one long session.

118
00:08:48,867 --> 00:08:50,109
Now it's your turn.

119
00:08:50,109 --> 00:08:52,006
Practise free on thewinegoat.

120
00:08:52,006 --> 00:08:57,237
com, with mixed questions and explanations, grape by grape and region by region.

121
00:08:57,237 --> 00:08:58,872
Good luck with your exam!
